    </paragraphs>

  </subsection>
<exercises>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>For any collection of vectors <m>v_1, \ldots, v_k</m> in <m>{\bf R}^n</m>, the zero vector in <m>{\bf R}^n</m> is a linear combination of <m>v_1, \ldots, v_k</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True: <m>0 = 0 v_1 + \cdots + 0 v_k</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>The vector <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> can be written as a linear combination of <m>\begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ -2\end{pmatrix}</m> in infinitely many ways.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True: the equation <m>x_1 \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 2\end{pmatrix} + x_2 \begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ -2\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> corresponds to the augmented matrix <m>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}2 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp -2 \amp 1\end{array}\right)</m>.  One step of row-reduction gives us <m>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}2 \amp -2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</m>, so the system is consistent and has a free variable, so it has infinitely many solutions.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>The vector <m>\begin{pmatrix}8 \\ 16 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> in the span of <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ 6\end{pmatrix}</m> and
      <m>\begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ -2 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True: row reduction quickly gives <m>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp -1 \amp 8 \\ 2 \amp -2 \amp 16 \\ 6 \amp -1 \amp 1\end{array}\right) \to \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp -1 \amp 8 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 5 \amp -47\end{array}\right)</m>.  We don't need to keep going to RREF to see that this augmented matrix has a pivot in every column except the rightmost column, so it is consistent.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>If <m>v_1, \ldots, v_k</m> are vectors in <m>{\bf R}^n</m>, then the equation <m>x_1 v_1 + \cdots + x_k v_k = 0</m> must be consistent.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>True: If we set <m>x_1</m> through <m>x_k</m> all equal to <m>0</m>, then
      <m>0v_1 + \cdots + 0v_k = 0</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>The plane <m>z=1</m> in <m>{\bf R}^3</m> is a span.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False: any span contains the zero vector but the plane <m>z=1</m> does not contain the zero vector, so it is not a span.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="yes">
      <p>The three vectors
        <m>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \ v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix},</m> and <m>v_3 = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>
        span <m>{\bf R}^3</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        True.  If <m>b</m> is any vector in <m>{\bf R}^3</m>, then the
        vector equation <m>x_1 v_1 + x_2 v_2 + x_3 v_3 = b</m> corresponds to the augmented matrix
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \amp \vert \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp b \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp \vert\end{array}\right) \xrightarrow[]{RREF}
              \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp * \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp * \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp *\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
        From the RREF we see that the augmented matrix cannot have a pivot in the rightmost column, so the system is consistent no matter what <m>b</m> is.  In other words, every <m>b</m> in <m>{\bf R}^3</m> is in <m>\Span\{v_1, v_2, v_3\}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>If <m>u</m>, <m>v</m>, and <m>w</m> are nonzero vectors in <m>{\bf R}^2</m>,
      then Span<m>\{u, v, w\}</m> is <m>{\bf R}^2</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  Take <m>v_1=v_2=v_3=\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Many other counterexamples
      possible.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement correct="no">
      <p>The line <m>y = 2x + 1</m> is a span of some number of vectors in
        <m>{\bf R}^2</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>False.  The span of any number of vectors must include the origin, but the
      line <m>y = 2x + 1</m> does not go through the origin.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose <m>v</m> is a vector in <m>{\bf R}^n</m>, and consider the set of all linear combinations of <m>v</m>.  What geometric shape(s) can this be?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>If <m>v</m> is the zero vector, then the set of all linear combinations of <m>v</m> is just the zero vector, since <m>c(0)=0</m> no matter what the scalar <m>c</m> is.</p>
      <p>If <m>v</m> is not the zero vector, then the set of all linear combinations of <m>v</m> is a line.  More specifically, it is the line through the origin and <m>v</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the following two vectors which characterize two colors in terms of Red, Green, and Blue.
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}180 \\ 50 \\ 200\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}100 \\ 150 \\ 100\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>
      </p>
      <image source="images/2_1-colors-1.png">
        <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
        <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
      </image>
      <p>For which <m>h</m> can we get <m>\begin{pmatrix}116 \\ 130 \\ h\end{pmatrix}</m> as a combination of those two colors?</p>
      <image source="images/2_1-colors-2.png">
        <shortdescription><!-- FILL: concise image description --></shortdescription>
        <description><p><!-- FILL: complete image description --></p></description>
      </image>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We put everything into an augmented matrix and row reduce.
        <md>
          <mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}180 \amp 100 \amp 116</mrow>
          <mrow>50 \amp 150 \amp 130</mrow>
          <mrow>200 \amp 100 \amp h\end{array}\right)
        \amp
        \xrightarrow[\textup{then } R_1 = \frac{R_1}{50}]{R_1 \leftrightarrow R_2}
        \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 3 \amp 13/5</mrow>
          <mrow>180 \amp 100 \amp 116</mrow>
          <mrow>200 \amp 100 \amp h\end{array}\right)
        \xrightarrow[R_3 = R_3 - 200 R_1]{R_2 = R_2 - 180 R_1}
        \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 3 \amp 13/5</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp -440 \amp -352</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp -500 \amp h-520\end{array}\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  \xrightarrow[]{R_3 = R_3 - (500/440)R_2}
        \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 3 \amp 13/5</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp -440 \amp -352</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp h-120\end{array}\right)</mrow>
        </md>.
        This system is consistent if and only if <m>h = 120</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the system of equations given by
        <md><mrow>5x_1 - x_2 + 5x_3 = 2</mrow></md>
        <md><mrow>-x_1 - 8x_3 = 4</mrow></md>
        <md><mrow>2x_1 + x_2 + x_3 = 0</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Write the system of equations as a vector equation.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p><m>x_1 \begin{pmatrix}5 \\ -1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix} + x_2 \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} + x_3 \begin{pmatrix}5 \\ -8 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}
            = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 4 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}.</m></p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Solve the vector equation, and use your solution to
          write the target vector in your equation as a linear combination
          of the other vectors.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We row-reduce:
          <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}5 \amp -1 \amp 5 \amp 2 \\ -1 \amp 0 \amp -8 \amp 4 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
                \xrightarrow[]{RREF} \left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 4/5 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -3/5\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
          This gives <m>x_1 = 4/5</m>, <m>x_2 = -1</m>, and <m>x_3 = -3/5</m>. Therefore,
          <md><mrow>\frac{4}{5} \begin{pmatrix}5 \\ -1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}
                - \frac{3}{5} \begin{pmatrix}5 \\ -8 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 4 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Write nonzero vectors <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m> in <m>{\bf R}^2</m> so
          that <m>\textup{Span}\{v_1, v_2\}</m> is a line.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Many examples possible.  For example
          <md><mrow>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}, \quad v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Write nonzero vectors <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m> in <m>{\bf R}^2</m> so
          that <m>\textup{Span}\{v_1, v_2\}</m> is the line <m>y = -4x</m> (where we use
          the coordinates <m>(x, y)</m> to label points in <m>{\bf R}^2</m>).</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We can choose any two nonzero vectors that are on the
              line <m>y = -4x</m>, which is the span of <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -4\end{pmatrix}</m>.  For example,
          <md><mrow>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ -4\end{pmatrix}, \quad v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ -8\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>v_3 = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>.
      Describe <m>\textup{Span}\{v_1, v_2, v_3\}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The vector <m>v_3</m> is a linear combination of <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m> since
        <m>v_1 + v_2 = v_3</m>, so <m>\textup{Span}\{v_1, v_2, v_3\}</m> is the same
        as <m>\textup{Span}\{v_1, v_2\}</m>.  The span of <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m> is the
        <m>xz</m>-plane of <m>{\bf R}^3</m>, since if <m>x_1</m> and <m>x_2</m> are any real
        numbers then <m>x_1 v_1 + x_2 v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}x_1 \\ 0 \\ x_2\end{pmatrix}</m>.  For an
        illustration, see this <url href="https://textbooks.math.gatech.edu/ila/1553/demos/spans.html?v1=1,0,0&amp;v2=0,0,1&amp;target=1,0,1&amp;range=3">graphic</url>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find all solutions to the vector equation
        <md><mrow>x_1 \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ -4 \\ 1\end{pmatrix} + x_2 \begin{pmatrix}4 \\ 1 \\ -2\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}2 \\ 14 \\ -7\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        If there are
        no solutions, justify why the vector equation is inconsistent.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}2 \amp 4 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>-4 \amp 1 \amp 14</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp -2 \amp -7\end{array}\right)
          \amp  \xrightarrow[]{R_1=R_1/2} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 2 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>-4 \amp 1 \amp 14</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp -2 \amp -7\end{array}\right)
        \xrightarrow[R_3=R_3-R_1]{R_2=R_2+4R_1} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 2 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 9 \amp 18</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp -4 \amp -8\end{array}\right)</mrow>
          <mrow></mrow>
          <mrow>\amp  \xrightarrow[R_3=R_3/-4]{R_2=R_2/9} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 2 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp 2\end{array}\right)
         \xrightarrow[R_1=R_1-2R_2]{R_3=R_3-R_2} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp -3</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)</mrow>
        </md>.
        Therefore, <m>x_1= \ -3</m> \ \ and \ <m>x_2=2</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Supppose we are asked to find a solution to the linear system of
        equations corresponding to the augmented matrix
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rrr|r}1 \amp -2 \amp 3 \amp -1 \\ 2 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\ -1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \amp 4\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Rewrite the problem in terms of a vector equation.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Find a solution to the vector equation
          <md><mrow>x_1 \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ -1\end{pmatrix} + x_2 \begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 0 \\ 2\end{pmatrix} + x_3 \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 1 \\ 3\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 3 \\ 4\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>Rewrite the problem as a question about linear
          combinations.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Write <m>\begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 2 \\ 3 \\ 4\end{pmatrix}</m> as a linear combination of
            <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 2 \\ -1\end{pmatrix}</m>, <m>\begin{pmatrix}-2 \\ 0 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m>, and <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 1 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find all values of <m>b</m> (if there are any) so that <m>\begin{pmatrix}b \\ -3 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> is a
      linear combination of <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 4\end{pmatrix}</m>, and write
      <m>\begin{pmatrix}b \\ -3 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> as a linear combination of <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> and
      <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 4\end{pmatrix}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We set up an augmented matrix and solve for <m>b</m> so that the
          corresponding system is consistent.  We will swap the first and
          third rows to make the math easier, but also to make it easier to
          solve for <m>b</m>.
        <md>
          <mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}3 \amp 1 \amp b</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp 0 \amp -3</mrow>
          <mrow>2 \amp 4 \amp 2\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[\textup{then } R_1 = R_1 / 2]{R_1 \leftrightarrow R_3}
            \amp
              \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 2 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>1 \amp 0 \amp -3</mrow>
          <mrow>3 \amp 1 \amp h\end{array}\right)
              \xrightarrow[R_3 = R_3 - 3R_1]{R_2 = R_2 - R_1}
              \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 2 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp -2 \amp -4</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp -5 \amp h-3\end{array}\right)</mrow>
          <mrow>\xrightarrow[]{R_2 = (-1/2)R_2}
            \amp
              \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 2 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp -5 \amp h-3\end{array}\right)
              \xrightarrow[]{R_3 = R_3 + 5R_2}
              \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 2 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp h+7\end{array}\right)</mrow>
        </md>.
        The system is consistent when there is no pivot in the right column,
          so <m>h+7=0</m>, thus <m>h = -7</m>.  To finish the problem, we need to solve
        <md><mrow>x_1 \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix} + x_2 \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 4\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-7 \\ -3 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        For this, we
          use the work that was already done above and do one more row
          operation.
        <md>
          <mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 2 \amp 1</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[]{R_1 = R_1 - 2R_2}
            \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp -3</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 1 \amp 2</mrow>
          <mrow>0 \amp 0 \amp 0\end{array}\right), \qquad x_1 = -3, \ x_2 = 2</mrow>
        </md>.
        <md><mrow>-3 \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 1 \\ 2\end{pmatrix} + 2 \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 4\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}-7 \\ -3 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Let <m>v_1 = \begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 0 \\ 2\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>v_2 = \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 1 \\ 3\end{pmatrix}</m>.  Write a vector <m>w</m>
      in <m>{\bf R}^3</m> that is <em>not</em> in <m>\textup{Span}\{v_1, v_2\}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Since <m>\textup{Span}\{v_1, v_2\}</m> is the set of all linear
          combinations of <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m>, we need to find a vector <m>w</m> that
          is not a linear combination of <m>v_1</m> and <m>v_2</m>.  In other words, we
          need to find a <m>w = \begin{pmatrix}a \\ b \\ c\end{pmatrix}</m> so that the vector equation
          <m>x_1v_1 + x_2 v_2 = w</m> is inconsistent.  We can put this in
          augmented matrix form and row-reduce.
        <md><mrow>\left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp a \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp b \\ 2 \amp 3 \amp c\end{array}\right)
            \xrightarrow[R_3 = R_3 - 3R_2]{R_3 = R_3 - 2 R_1} \left(\begin{array}{rr|r}1 \amp 0 \amp a \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp b \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp c-2a-3b\end{array}\right)</mrow></md>.
        The system will be inconsistent precisely when the final column has
          a pivot, which is when <m>c - 2a - 3b \neq 0</m>.  For example, the
          vector <m>\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ 1 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m> will make the system inconsistent since
          <m>1-2(1)-3(1) = -4 \neq 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>Alternatively, we could have done this problem through trial and
        error.  Since the system of equations is
        <m>x_1 = a, \ x_2 = b, \ 2x_1 + 3x_2 = c</m>, one vector that will make
        this inconsistent is <m>\begin{pmatrix}a \\ b \\ c\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}0 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{pmatrix}</m>, since that
        requires <m>x_1 = 0</m> and <m>x_2 = 0</m> but <m>2x_1 + 3x_2 = 1</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find all values of <m>c</m> and <m>d</m> so that
      <m>\textup{Span}\left\{\begin{pmatrix}1 \\ c\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}d \\ 2\end{pmatrix}\right\}</m> is not
      <m>{\bf R}^2</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Neither of the vectors given is the zero vector, so their span
          is at least a line.  The only way their span is just a line is if
          they are collinear.  Therefore, the vectors will span <m>{\bf R}^2</m>
          unless one is a scalar multiple of the other, which means there is
          some (nonzero) <m>k</m> so that <m>d = 1k</m> and <m>2 = ck</m>, which means
        <md><mrow>k = d = 2/c</mrow></md>.
        Therefore, <m>c</m> is any nonzero real number and <m>d = 2/c</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the set <m>V</m> of all vectors in <m>{\bf R}^3</m> of the form
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}3x_1-x_2 \\ 2x_1+5x_2 \\ -10x_2\end{pmatrix} \quad (x_1, \ x_2
          \textup{ real})</mrow></md>.
        Write <m>V</m> as a span of vectors in <m>{\bf R}^3</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Unwrapping the general form of a vector in <m>V</m>, we see
        <md><mrow>\begin{pmatrix}3x_1-x_2 \\ 2x_1+5x_2 \\ -10x_2\end{pmatrix} =
            \begin{pmatrix}3x_1 \\ 2x_1 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}-x_2 \\ 5x_2 \\ -10x_2\end{pmatrix}
            = x_1 \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix} + \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 5 \\ -10\end{pmatrix}</mrow></md>.
        Therefore, the vectors in <m>V</m> are simply all linear combinations
          of <m>\begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 5 \\ -10\end{pmatrix}</m>.  In other words,
        <md><mrow>V = \textup{Span}\left\{ \begin{pmatrix}3 \\ 2 \\ 0\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}-1 \\ 5 \\ -10\end{pmatrix}.\right\}</mrow></md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</exercises>

</section>
